  "segments": [
    {
      "start": 0.0,
      "end": 29.66,
      "text": " Cut here. Tear up here. How does an onion reach your eyes? The knife damages the onion's tissue, starting a chain of chemical reactions. One step uses an enzyme called lachrymatory factor synthase. It helps make a volatile compound called the lachrymatory factor. Volatile means it can enter the air. That compound causes the familiar eye irritation associated with chopping onions. So the slice and the irritation"
    },
    {
      "start": 29.66,
      "end": 38.74,
      "text": " happen in different places, connected by an airborne chemical. The knife never touched your eye, it started a reaction that could reach it."
    },
    {
      "start": 38.74,
      "end": 38.86,
      "text": " . . . . . . . . . ."
    }
  ],
  "text": "Cut here. Tear up here. How does an onion reach your eyes? The knife damages the onion's tissue, starting a chain of chemical reactions. One step uses an enzyme called lachrymatory factor synthase. It helps make a volatile compound called the lachrymatory factor. Volatile means it can enter the air. That compound causes the familiar eye irritation associated with chopping onions. So the slice and the irritation happen in different places, connected by an airborne chemical. The knife never touched your eye, it started a reaction that could reach it. . . . . . . . . . ."
}
